  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a westerly flow aloft prevailing across the \nWestern High Plains downstream of weak ridging aloft transitioning \nslowly east through the Intermountain West. Near the surface, a \nbroad area of high pressure is drifting east through the Great Lakes.\n\nMuch drier conditions return to western Kansas through the weekend \nas the SREF indicates ridging aloft slowly building as it edges \neastward across the Great Basin, lending to a fairly weak west-\nnorthwest flow aloft across the Western High Plains. Meanwhile, \ndeveloping southerlies Saturday will be slow to tap into moisture, \ndoing little to introduce/increase any instability to the area. \n\nSeasonal temperatures are likely tonight as slowly departing surface\nhigh pressure across the Great Lakes does little to erode the cooler\nair mass within easterly upsloping across the high plains of western\nKansas. The latest HREF shows a 70-90% probability of temperatures\ndropping below 50F in west central Kansas and much of southwest Kansas\nto 50-70% probability of temperatures slipping below 55F in south\ncentral Kansas, so look for lows generally down into the 40s(F),\nespecially where skies remain mostly clear. The lower 50s(F) cannot\nbe ruled out in south central Kansas where prevailing low level stratus\njust south of the Oklahoma line potentially edging back north a bit.\nRebounding temperatures are likely Saturday as developing southerlies\nincrease throughout the day as lee side troughing deepens in eastern\nColorado. This will draw slightly warmer air northward into western\nKansas, pushing H85 temperatures a little above 15C in central Kansas\nto near 20C near the Colorado line. Expect widespread afternoon highs\nin the 70s(F) with the HREF painting a 70-90% probability of temperatures\nexceeding 70F. Similar temperatures are forecast Sunday with a weak\nfront projected to push southward through western Kansas early morning,\nhelping cap the warming trend briefly.",
